1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:04,110
That gold baby ring in your drawer: what would a jeweler pay today?

2
00:00:04,110 --> 00:00:06,300
640,000 won per don, as of October 26.

3
00:00:07,100 --> 00:00:10,690
Measured on the Korea Exchange gold market,

4
00:00:10,880 --> 00:00:14,945
it peaked at 1.01 million won a don on January 29.

5
00:00:14,945 --> 00:00:17,790
Today it's about 640,000 there too.

6
00:00:18,590 --> 00:00:20,242
Down 37% in nine months.

7
00:00:20,242 --> 00:00:23,820
As if one in every three gold rings simply vanished.

8
00:00:24,630 --> 00:00:28,139
And international gold slipped below 4,000 dollars an ounce,

9
00:00:28,139 --> 00:00:29,660
about 31 grams, last week.

10
00:00:30,450 --> 00:00:33,170
So should you sell now, or hold on?

11
00:00:33,980 --> 00:00:36,030
Stay to the end for three answers.

12
00:00:36,220 --> 00:00:39,050
One: who dragged gold down 37%.

13
00:00:39,250 --> 00:00:42,330
Two: who makes money whether gold rises or falls.

14
00:00:42,530 --> 00:00:44,935
Three: what Warren Buffett bought instead of gold,

15
00:00:44,935 --> 00:00:47,340
and three questions to decide about your own ring.

16
00:00:48,150 --> 00:00:49,776
But one number is odd.

17
00:00:49,776 --> 00:00:54,136
International gold is down about 29% from its January high,

18
00:00:54,136 --> 00:00:56,870
yet Korean exchange gold is down 37%.

19
00:00:57,070 --> 00:00:58,670
Why did Korea fall further?

20
00:00:59,470 --> 00:01:01,500
Let's rewind to this January.

21
00:01:02,300 --> 00:01:04,648
On the Korea Exchange gold market,

22
00:01:04,648 --> 00:01:07,756
one don went from 783,000 won on January 2 to

23
00:01:07,756 --> 00:01:11,140
1.01 million on January 29: up 29% in four weeks.

24
00:01:11,940 --> 00:01:14,306
On January 26 international gold topped 5,000

25
00:01:14,306 --> 00:01:16,199
dollars an ounce for the first time,

26
00:01:16,199 --> 00:01:18,250
and by late January it was above 5,500.

27
00:01:18,460 --> 00:01:20,432
Party guests were quietly reaching for cash

28
00:01:20,432 --> 00:01:21,900
envelopes instead of gold rings.

29
00:01:22,700 --> 00:01:23,669
And that was the top.

30
00:01:23,669 --> 00:01:25,006
On the same exchange measure,

31
00:01:25,006 --> 00:01:27,175
one don was 900,000 won at the end of February,

32
00:01:27,175 --> 00:01:28,420
740,000 at the end of June,

33
00:01:28,620 --> 00:01:31,540
and 640,000 by October 26.

34
00:01:31,740 --> 00:01:32,836
A few pauses,

35
00:01:32,836 --> 00:01:36,040
but nine months of walking downstairs.

36
00:01:36,240 --> 00:01:38,870
So who pushed gold down like this?

37
00:01:39,660 --> 00:01:41,000
There are three suspects.

38
00:01:41,800 --> 00:01:45,007
Suspect one: interest. On September 16 the U.S.

39
00:01:45,007 --> 00:01:46,985
Federal Reserve raised rates,

40
00:01:46,985 --> 00:01:49,100
its first hike since July 2023.

41
00:01:49,300 --> 00:01:51,803
Its answer to oil prices and inflation climbing

42
00:01:51,803 --> 00:01:53,400
again on Middle East tensions.

43
00:01:54,200 --> 00:01:58,395
The 10-year Treasury yield hit 5.1% on September 23,

44
00:01:58,395 --> 00:02:00,250
the highest since 2007.

45
00:02:01,050 --> 00:02:04,330
Here's gold's weakness: it pays no interest at all.

46
00:02:04,530 --> 00:02:06,177
At late-September rates,

47
00:02:06,177 --> 00:02:09,745
10,000 dollars in Treasuries earns about 500 a year.

48
00:02:09,745 --> 00:02:12,490
Gold in a safe is the same a year later.

49
00:02:12,690 --> 00:02:14,616
Holding gold means giving up that interest,

50
00:02:14,616 --> 00:02:16,810
so higher rates make gold more expensive to hold.

51
00:02:17,610 --> 00:02:21,013
Suspect two: the money that piled in during January.

52
00:02:21,013 --> 00:02:23,958
Analysts say short-term money that chased the

53
00:02:23,958 --> 00:02:26,510
rally made the fall steeper as it left.

54
00:02:26,710 --> 00:02:29,327
Everyone who crowded into the elevator on the

55
00:02:29,327 --> 00:02:31,420
way up tried to squeeze out at once.

56
00:02:32,230 --> 00:02:35,234
Then last week something stranger happened: U.S.

57
00:02:35,234 --> 00:02:37,550
yields fell, and gold fell with them.

58
00:02:37,750 --> 00:02:41,587
One analyst called it the first such joint drop

59
00:02:41,587 --> 00:02:43,220
since December 2008.

60
00:02:44,020 --> 00:02:46,538
Enter suspect three. When U.S.

61
00:02:46,538 --> 00:02:48,805
tech stocks slid last week,

62
00:02:48,805 --> 00:02:53,170
investors using borrowed money suddenly needed cash.

63
00:02:53,370 --> 00:02:56,252
In moments like that, gold is often sold first,

64
00:02:56,252 --> 00:02:58,520
because it turns into cash instantly.

65
00:02:58,720 --> 00:03:01,388
Not because anyone stopped liking gold.

66
00:03:01,388 --> 00:03:05,630
It was just the fastest thing in the wallet to turn into cash.

67
00:03:06,430 --> 00:03:10,430
Part one: let's line up the three suspects again.

68
00:03:10,630 --> 00:03:14,760
The spring slide came largely from January's crowd leaving,

69
00:03:14,760 --> 00:03:17,280
higher interest weighed in the fall,

70
00:03:17,280 --> 00:03:21,340
and last week's plunge added sellers who needed cash fast.

71
00:03:22,140 --> 00:03:24,789
So why did Korean gold fall further?

72
00:03:24,789 --> 00:03:27,438
The local price is the global price,

73
00:03:27,438 --> 00:03:30,970
times the won-dollar rate, plus a local premium.

74
00:03:31,160 --> 00:03:34,687
An April report found Korean gold averaged 7 to

75
00:03:34,687 --> 00:03:36,714
10% above the global price,

76
00:03:36,714 --> 00:03:38,740
and up to 20% on some days.

77
00:03:38,940 --> 00:03:43,225
So the 8-point gap comes from the exchange rate and the premium.

78
00:03:43,225 --> 00:03:44,698
Hard to split exactly,

79
00:03:44,698 --> 00:03:47,711
but a premium usually builds when gold is hot

80
00:03:47,711 --> 00:03:49,720
and fades when the heat cools.

81
00:03:50,530 --> 00:03:53,908
So who makes money whether gold rises or falls?

82
00:03:53,908 --> 00:03:57,143
The clue sits where it's busy either way: the

83
00:03:57,143 --> 00:03:59,730
price board at a local jewelry shop.

84
00:04:00,530 --> 00:04:01,429
On October 26,

85
00:04:01,429 --> 00:04:03,996
the price board of wholesaler Korea Gold

86
00:04:03,996 --> 00:04:08,040
Exchange showed 750,000 won to buy one don and 640,000 to sell.

87
00:04:08,040 --> 00:04:11,570
Not to be confused with the Korea Exchange gold market.

88
00:04:11,770 --> 00:04:14,550
Same day, same gold, two prices.

89
00:04:15,350 --> 00:04:18,470
A 110,000 won gap. Nearly 70,000 of it is VAT,

90
00:04:18,470 --> 00:04:20,911
since the buying price includes 10%.

91
00:04:20,911 --> 00:04:23,760
The rest goes to the people in the middle.

92
00:04:23,960 --> 00:04:27,988
So how much must gold rise just to break even if you buy today?

93
00:04:27,988 --> 00:04:30,930
750,000 divided by 640,000 is 1.17: a 17% rise

94
00:04:30,930 --> 00:04:32,720
just to get your money back.

95
00:04:32,930 --> 00:04:36,350
Add a craftsmanship fee for a baby ring and the bar is even higher.

96
00:04:37,140 --> 00:04:40,327
Jewelers and wholesalers earn on that gap,

97
00:04:40,327 --> 00:04:43,438
minus VAT, every time gold changes hands,

98
00:04:43,438 --> 00:04:46,170
up or down, as long as people trade.

99
00:04:46,370 --> 00:04:47,741
In last year's rally,

100
00:04:47,741 --> 00:04:50,809
importer Korea Gold Exchange reportedly doubled

101
00:04:50,809 --> 00:04:52,898
its revenue to 7.7 trillion won.

102
00:04:52,898 --> 00:04:54,269
Revenue isn't profit,

103
00:04:54,269 --> 00:04:57,860
but more gold changing hands keeps the crossroads busy.

104
00:04:58,060 --> 00:04:58,959
That's part two.

105
00:04:58,959 --> 00:05:02,051
The steadiest money goes not to those who predict gold,

106
00:05:02,051 --> 00:05:04,580
but to those standing where it changes hands.

107
00:05:05,380 --> 00:05:08,348
Where you buy changes the price structure.

108
00:05:08,348 --> 00:05:10,822
A jewelry shop sells physical gold,

109
00:05:10,822 --> 00:05:12,730
with VAT and a wide spread.

110
00:05:12,930 --> 00:05:15,284
The Korea Exchange gold market trades in single

111
00:05:15,284 --> 00:05:16,987
grams through a brokerage account,

112
00:05:16,987 --> 00:05:19,040
with no VAT unless you withdraw the bars.

113
00:05:19,240 --> 00:05:23,250
Not which is better: just know the price is built differently.

114
00:05:24,050 --> 00:05:28,000
One famous investor openly disliked gold: Warren Buffett.

115
00:05:28,800 --> 00:05:30,096
In February 2012,

116
00:05:30,096 --> 00:05:33,220
Buffett wrote to shareholders about gold.

117
00:05:33,420 --> 00:05:35,841
All the world's gold, about 170,000 tons,

118
00:05:35,841 --> 00:05:39,443
melted together would make a cube about 68 feet on each side.

119
00:05:39,443 --> 00:05:41,510
It would fit in a baseball infield.

120
00:05:41,710 --> 00:05:44,480
At the time, it was worth 9.6 trillion dollars.

121
00:05:45,280 --> 00:05:48,373
What else could that money buy? All U.S.

122
00:05:48,373 --> 00:05:50,771
cropland, plus 16 Exxon Mobils,

123
00:05:50,771 --> 00:05:53,400
with a trillion dollars left over.

124
00:05:53,590 --> 00:05:56,208
He called that trillion walking-around money.

125
00:05:56,208 --> 00:05:58,127
After a shopping spree like that,

126
00:05:58,127 --> 00:05:59,640
no sense feeling strapped.

127
00:06:00,450 --> 00:06:01,925
Picture a century from now.

128
00:06:01,925 --> 00:06:04,219
The farmland will have grown endless corn,

129
00:06:04,219 --> 00:06:05,147
wheat and cotton,

130
00:06:05,147 --> 00:06:08,370
and the companies will have piled up profits and dividends.

131
00:06:08,570 --> 00:06:12,520
The cube? Same size, still producing nothing.

132
00:06:13,320 --> 00:06:15,020
And then he added:

133
00:06:15,220 --> 00:06:16,965
You can fondle the cube,

134
00:06:16,965 --> 00:06:18,710
but it will not respond.

135
00:06:19,510 --> 00:06:22,970
Gold did rise afterward: from 1,750 dollars an

136
00:06:22,970 --> 00:06:26,130
ounce when he wrote to around 4,000 today.

137
00:06:26,330 --> 00:06:29,259
But his point wasn't whether gold goes up.

138
00:06:29,259 --> 00:06:31,352
Gold earns nothing on its own,

139
00:06:31,352 --> 00:06:34,490
so it rises only if someone pays more for it.

140
00:06:34,680 --> 00:06:37,927
So Berkshire Hathaway bought things that work on their own,

141
00:06:37,927 --> 00:06:39,359
like farmland: businesses.

142
00:06:39,359 --> 00:06:41,340
That's the first half of part three.

143
00:06:42,150 --> 00:06:44,805
Yet today the steadiest gold buyers are some of

144
00:06:44,805 --> 00:06:47,290
the most conservative institutions on earth.

145
00:06:48,090 --> 00:06:49,122
Central banks.

146
00:06:49,122 --> 00:06:51,776
According to the World Gold Council,

147
00:06:51,776 --> 00:06:55,240
they bought 863 tonnes of gold last year alone.

148
00:06:55,440 --> 00:06:58,120
More than 1,000 tonnes a year in each of the three years before.

149
00:06:58,920 --> 00:07:01,301
Even as gold falls, the buying continues.

150
00:07:01,301 --> 00:07:04,031
Goldman Sachs estimates central banks bought 91

151
00:07:04,031 --> 00:07:06,470
tonnes a month over the last three months.

152
00:07:06,670 --> 00:07:10,840
Before 2022 it was 17 tonnes a month: more than five times as much.

153
00:07:11,640 --> 00:07:12,963
The reason is insurance.

154
00:07:12,963 --> 00:07:15,498
Gold doesn't rely on any government's promise,

155
00:07:15,498 --> 00:07:18,860
so it stays even if something happens to the dollar or bonds.

156
00:07:19,060 --> 00:07:21,483
Buffett admitted this too: a century from now,

157
00:07:21,483 --> 00:07:24,170
people will still rush to gold when they're afraid.

158
00:07:24,370 --> 00:07:27,125
Buying gold is buying insurance; buying a

159
00:07:27,125 --> 00:07:28,939
company is hiring a worker.

160
00:07:28,939 --> 00:07:31,560
They're different things to begin with.

161
00:07:32,370 --> 00:07:34,817
So how should you read a gold outlook?

162
00:07:34,817 --> 00:07:37,264
Rather than guessing tomorrow's price,

163
00:07:37,264 --> 00:07:39,260
watch the suspects we just met.

164
00:07:39,450 --> 00:07:42,935
Higher Treasury yields make gold costlier to hold,

165
00:07:42,935 --> 00:07:47,255
a higher won-dollar rate and premium tend to lift Korean gold,

166
00:07:47,255 --> 00:07:50,740
and central-bank buying acts as a supporting hand.

167
00:07:51,550 --> 00:07:54,196
Now, back to the ring in the drawer.

168
00:07:54,196 --> 00:07:58,460
Part three: three questions to decide about your own gold.

169
00:07:59,260 --> 00:08:02,085
One: do you need this money within a year?

170
00:08:02,085 --> 00:08:05,179
No one knows which way gold will move or when,

171
00:08:05,179 --> 00:08:07,466
so decide by the date you need it,

172
00:08:07,466 --> 00:08:08,610
not by the price.

173
00:08:08,810 --> 00:08:11,998
Two: would you buy gold again later?

174
00:08:11,998 --> 00:08:16,160
Then you'd face that 17% hurdle all over again.

175
00:08:16,360 --> 00:08:19,122
Three: what share of your wealth is in gold?

176
00:08:19,122 --> 00:08:21,193
Insurance is about having enough,

177
00:08:21,193 --> 00:08:22,700
not as much as possible.

178
00:08:23,490 --> 00:08:24,307
If you do sell,

179
00:08:24,307 --> 00:08:26,813
compare buy-back prices at two or three shops,

180
00:08:26,813 --> 00:08:29,700
and remember the craftsmanship fee doesn't come back.

181
00:08:29,900 --> 00:08:32,043
No one knows if gold goes up or down tomorrow.

182
00:08:32,043 --> 00:08:35,070
But you can answer these three questions from your own situation.

183
00:08:35,270 --> 00:08:38,292
And if that ring holds the memory of a first birthday,

184
00:08:38,292 --> 00:08:40,530
its value may not be on any price board.

185
00:08:41,330 --> 00:08:44,336
One last thing. You can't score gold.

186
00:08:44,336 --> 00:08:45,880
It has no earnings.

187
00:08:46,070 --> 00:08:49,257
Companies have earnings, so they can be scored.

188
00:08:49,257 --> 00:08:52,308
ValueNote scores 600-plus companies daily out

189
00:08:52,308 --> 00:08:54,816
of 100 by great investors' standards,

190
00:08:54,816 --> 00:08:58,410
with separate scores for earning power and for price.

191
00:08:58,610 --> 00:09:01,940
A good company and a good price are two different questions.

192
00:09:02,740 --> 00:09:05,377
Want more stories behind the numbers? Subscribe,

193
00:09:05,377 --> 00:09:07,080
like and turn on notifications.

194
00:09:07,280 --> 00:09:10,995
Watch our episode on the Japanese companies Warren Buffett picked,

195
00:09:10,995 --> 00:09:11,220
too.

196
00:09:11,420 --> 00:09:13,252
How much gold is sitting in your drawer?

197
00:09:13,252 --> 00:09:15,450
Tell us in the comments. Thank you for watching.
